\section*{Decidability of Rational Points over Number Fields}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 004; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that rational solvability of integer-coefficient polynomials with an unrestricted number of variables is undecidable.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
With $n$ variable and integral polynomial coefficients explicitly encoded, the rational Diophantine decision claim is
\[
 \{f\in\mathbb Z[X_1,\ldots,X_n]:\exists x\in\mathbb Q^n,\ f(x)=0\}
 \quad\text{is undecidable}.
\]
There is no algorithm giving the correct yes/no answer for every such input; the coefficient ring is $\mathbb Z$, but sought solutions lie in $\mathbb Q$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 004; 2026-09-24), integer solvability is represented by an effective sequence of finite rational tests. Elliptic indices, odd valuation contacts, and a height argument force an ordinary integer witness when every test succeeds; a hypothetical rational decision procedure would then decide the integer problem by two concurrent searches.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The claimed undecidability over $\mathbb Q$ rules out a general algorithm for rational-point existence over number fields: an integer polynomial $f$ is sent to its affine zero locus $V(f)$ over $\mathbb Q$, whose rational points are exactly the rational solutions of $f=0$. The result already obstructs the requested procedure at $k=\mathbb Q$; it does not assert undecidability separately for each other fixed number field.

\subsection{Formal verification}
No Lean formalization is listed at this snapshot for manuscript 1 (family 004; 2026-09-24).

